WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.616
Fraud screening runs each payment through a list of rules.

2
00:00:06.616 --> 00:00:09.519
Here, 900,000 cents trips the amount limit.

3
00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:12.155
A rule here is a value, like a number.

4
00:00:12.155 --> 00:00:16.615
It sits in a variable, it goes into a list, and it gets passed around.

5
00:00:16.615 --> 00:00:19.737
A function written as a value is called a lambda.

6
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:24.627
A lambda can also use a variable from outside its braces.

7
00:00:24.627 --> 00:00:26.181
This rule reads limit.

8
00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:29.609
It carries that value along, wherever the rule goes.

9
00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:33.100
Code plus the variables it captured is called a closure.

10
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.292
Now capture a var, one that changes.

11
00:00:36.292 --> 00:00:40.676
makeCounter makes count, and returns a lambda that adds one to it.

12
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:44.418
When makeCounter returns, its local variables are gone.

13
00:00:44.418 --> 00:00:46.549
But the lambda still needs count.

14
00:00:46.549 --> 00:00:47.969
Where does count live?

15
00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:55.353
The compiler moves count into a small object on the heap, a Ref box with one plain field.

16
00:00:55.353 --> 00:00:57.239
The lambda points at the box.

17
00:00:57.433 --> 00:01:01.259
Call the lambda three times, and the box counts one, two, three.

18
00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:05.297
Now hand that lambda to two threads at once.

19
00:01:05.297 --> 00:01:09.981
Each ++count is three steps: read the field, add one, write it back.

20
00:01:10.167 --> 00:01:11.845
Both threads read 41.

21
00:01:11.845 --> 00:01:13.764
What ends up in the box?

22
00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:15.604
42.

23
00:01:15.604 --> 00:01:18.486
Both add one, and both write 42.

24
00:01:18.486 --> 00:01:21.369
Two increments, and one is lost.

25
00:01:21.567 --> 00:01:31.963
In the lesson's run, four threads each added 250,000, and the total came out 669,421, not a million.

26
00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:34.332
Nothing in the source looks shared.

27
00:01:34.332 --> 00:01:35.940
The compiler made the box.

28
00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:40.208
The fix: let each task count its own, and add the results at the end.

29
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:47.510
Java refuses to capture a local that changes, so you build the box yourself, a one-element array.

30
00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:50.933
Kotlin builds the same box for you, without a word.

31
00:01:51.733 --> 00:01:54.838
The rule: a lambda keeps the variables it uses.

32
00:01:54.838 --> 00:01:59.000
A captured var is one shared box, so keep other threads off it.

33
00:01:59.233 --> 00:02:00.116
Quick check.

34
00:02:00.116 --> 00:02:01.807
Call makeCounter twice.

35
00:02:01.807 --> 00:02:04.160
Do the two counters share a box?

36
00:02:05.733 --> 00:02:05.958
No.

37
00:02:05.958 --> 00:02:10.760
Each call makes its own count, so each counter gets its own box.
